---
import { knownRelease, ZOTERO_MAJORS } from "../../../src/site/version.ts";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import {
  BASE,
  CATEGORIES,
  categoryOptions,
  LICENSE_HREF,
  loadData,
  REMOVE_PLUGIN_HREF,
  SITE_DOCS_HREF,
  SUPPORT_MARKS as MARKS,
} from "../data.ts";

const { plugins, zoteroRelease } = loadData();
// The categories that the home page's menu offers, the only ones a link can filter by.
const offered = new Set(categoryOptions(plugins).map(({ value }) => value));
---

<Base
  title="About: Zotdex"
  description="How Zotdex works: what the facts on a plugin page mean and what they don't tell you."
  path={`${BASE}about/`}
>
  <div class="hero">
    <h1>How Zotdex works</h1>
    <p class="intro">
      Every day, Zotdex reads each listed plugin's GitHub repository and releases. Here's what the facts on a plugin
      page mean, and what they don't tell you. The <a href={SITE_DOCS_HREF} data-site-docs>site docs</a> on GitHub have
      the full rules.
    </p>
  </div>
  <section class="card" id="declared" data-about="declared">
    <h2>Supported Zotero versions</h2>
    <p>
      Each plugin says which Zotero versions it supports, and Zotdex shows that range, with any corrections.{" "}
      <span class="mark" data-support="all" role="img" aria-label="check mark">{MARKS.all}</span> means it supports every
      release of that version, <span class="mark" data-support="some" role="img" aria-label="half circle">{MARKS.some}</span>{" "}
      some releases, and <span class="mark" data-support="none" role="img" aria-label="dash">{MARKS.none}</span> none. A plugin that doesn't say shows no mark. The range
      is what the author says, not a test result.
    </p>
    <p>This update counted these as the newest releases:</p>
    <ul class="about-list" role="list" data-newest-releases>
      {
        ZOTERO_MAJORS.map((major) => {
          const release = knownRelease(major, zoteroRelease);
          return <li data-major={major}>{release ? `Zotero ${major}: ${release}` : `Zotero ${major}: no known release`}</li>;
        })
      }
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="start-test" data-about="start-test">
    <h2>Startup check</h2>
    <p>
      Zotdex installs each plugin in a clean Zotero setup, once for each Zotero version the plugin says it supports, and
      waits 3 seconds. <span class="start" data-start="started" role="img" aria-label="dot">●</span> means Zotero lists it as active and it logged no
      startup error. <span class="start" data-start="start-error" role="img" aria-label="cross">✕</span> means it failed to load, didn't install, or
      didn't finish loading within 90 seconds. Passing doesn't mean the plugin works, because the check doesn't use any
      of its features, and the plugin's own code can change its result. Zotdex checks a plugin again when its file
      changes, after a failed check, and once a week, so a result is usually less than a week old.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="older-data" data-about="older-data">
    <h2>Older data</h2>
    <p>
      When Zotdex can't reach a plugin because of a time limit or a server problem, it keeps what it found in the past 7
      days and marks the plugin "Older data". A plugin it can't read for another reason, such as a deleted repository,
      shows "Not read".
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="reports" data-about="reports">
    <h2>User reports</h2>
    <p>
      Anyone with a GitHub account can report that a plugin works or is broken, with the buttons on its page. A bot
      checks each report, and Zotdex counts the ones that pass, keeping each person's latest for each Zotero version. The
      counts start over with each new release of the plugin, and they show what people found, not what Zotdex tested.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="descriptions" data-about="descriptions">
    <h2>Descriptions</h2>
    <p>
      A plugin's description comes from its own file or its GitHub page. If a plugin comes in several languages, Zotdex
      uses its English text. For a description that's mostly Chinese, Japanese, or Korean, Zotdex shows just the part in
      Latin letters, if there is one. The Details card shows both descriptions when a plugin has both.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="icons" data-about="icons">
    <h2>Icons and screenshots</h2>
    <p>
      A plugin's icon is the one its author made for Zotero's Plugins window. Zotdex doesn't look at icons before it shows
      them, and a maintainer can hide one. A screenshot comes from the plugin's owner, who asks for it with the link on
      the plugin's page. A maintainer looks at each one before it's shown.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="overrides" data-about="overrides">
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      When a fact from a plugin is wrong and its author can't or won't fix it, Zotdex can correct it, such as its name,
      description, or supported versions. A plugin's owner can also ask for a new name or description with the link on
      its page, and a maintainer reads each request. Each plugin page lists its corrections in the "Corrections by
      Zotdex" table, beside the plugin's own values.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="categories" data-about="categories">
    <h2>Categories</h2>
    <p>
      Zotdex sorts plugins into its own categories, from words in their names and descriptions plus some fixes by hand,
      so you can find plugins of one kind. A category can be wrong or missing, and plugin authors don't choose them.
    </p>
    <ul class="about-list" role="list" data-category-list>
      {
        CATEGORIES.map((category) => (
          <li>
            {offered.has(category.slug) ? <a href={`${BASE}?category=${category.slug}`}>{category.name}</a> : category.name}:{" "}
            {category.about}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="source" data-about="source">
    <h2>Source code</h2>
    <p>
      A lock means Zotdex found no obvious source code in the top folder of the plugin's repository. It only looks at
      file and folder names, not at the code, so it can be wrong either way, and a plugin without the lock isn't always
      open source. A plugin with the lock stays listed.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="paid-service" data-about="paid-service">
    <h2>Paid service</h2>
    <p>
      A card icon means the plugin's main use stops unless you pay its maker, or stops after a trial. A maintainer checks
      this by hand. A free plan with paid extras, a plugin that also works with another service's key, or a donation gets
      no mark. A plugin with no mark can still cost money, because new plugins get checked later.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="removing" data-about="removing">
    <h2>Removing a plugin</h2>
    <p>
      A maintainer removes a plugin when its owner asks, when it's spam, when its entry misleads people about what it is
      or who makes it, or when someone shows evidence that it causes harm. To ask, use the{" "}
      <a href={REMOVE_PLUGIN_HREF} data-remove-form>form for removing a plugin or reporting harm</a>, or the link on the
      plugin's page. A removed plugin stays off Zotdex.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="installing" data-about="installing">
    <h2>Installing a plugin</h2>
    <p>
      Save the plugin's .xpi file, then in Zotero go to Tools → Plugins and drag the file onto the Plugins window. In
      Firefox, right-click the download link and choose Save Link As.
    </p>
    <p>
      A plugin has full access to Zotero and your computer, and Zotdex doesn't test plugins' safety or features, so look
      into a plugin before you trust it with your library. A bot adds most new plugins, and a maintainer steps in when
      the bot flags one, mainly to keep out entries that mislead people about what a plugin is or who makes it.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="not-vetted" data-about="not-vetted">
    <h2>What Zotdex doesn't do</h2>
    <p>
      Zotdex is independent and unofficial, and it's not part of Zotero. It can't vet the plugins it lists, because one
      person runs it and any plugin can change with each release. It doesn't host plugin files: download links go to each
      author's own file.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="card" id="license" data-about="license">
    <h2>License</h2>
    <p>
      Zotdex's code is under the MIT License, and its data, categories, and own text are under CC0 1.0. Names,
      descriptions, icons, screenshots, and other work from plugin authors keep their authors' terms. The{" "}
      <a href={LICENSE_HREF} data-license-link>license page</a> gives the details, including the exceptions.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
